# Cursor Rules — Investment Questionnaire

Project: Node.js/Express investment questionnaire that turns a 7-section
form (A–G) into a financial model (revenue, costs, EBITDA, net profit,
required loan, NPV, IRR, PI, payback). Frontend is vanilla HTML/JS/CSS in
`public/`, backend logic is in `server.js`. Single endpoint: `POST /calculate`.

## Scope Discipline (highest priority)

- **Do only what was asked.** No refactors, renames, "improvements", new
  features, or unrelated edits unless explicitly requested.
- **No unilateral decisions.** When anything is ambiguous, inputs are
  missing, or there is a meaningful trade-off — ASK the user. Do not guess.
- **No "while I'm here" edits.** If you spot something else, mention it and
  ask whether to act; do not act first.
- Full policy lives in `.cursor/rules/scope-discipline.mdc`.

## Agent Roles & Workflow

Every request flows through three roles defined in `.cursor/rules/`:

1. **Lead** (`lead-agent.mdc`) — validates the task, runs a checklist
   (scope, contract, deps, acceptance criteria), asks clarifying questions
   if anything is unclear, then dispatches a concrete brief.
2. **Developer-Tester** (`developer-tester-agent.mdc`) — implements the
   smallest change that satisfies the brief (Phase 1: Develop), then
   verifies it against acceptance criteria, contract integrity, numerical
   sanity, edge cases, language policy, lints, and bookkeeping
   (Phase 2: Test). Reports PASS/FAIL per criterion back to the Lead.
3. **Lead (acceptance)** — confirms PASS on all criteria and summarises
   the result to the user.

Ambiguous tasks never reach the Developer-Tester. Untested changes are
never reported as complete.

## Language Policy (strict)

- Code, identifiers, comments, commit messages, rule files, CHANGELOG: **English**.
- User-facing strings (HTML, alerts, chart labels), `README.md`, domain
  copy: **Russian**. Russian month names and seasonality keys
  (`winter`/`spring`/`summer`/`autumn`) are part of the API contract — do
  not translate them.

## Stack & Boundaries

- Node.js 20 + Express 4, CORS enabled, static `public/`.
- No build step, no bundler, no TypeScript. Do not introduce them without
  an explicit request.
- Frontend dependencies (Chart.js, xlsx-js-style) are loaded via CDN — keep
  it that way.
- Default port `5000`, configurable via `PORT`. `npm start` -> `node server.js`.
- Do not change `package.json` `name`, `main`, or `start` without being asked.

## Data Contract (`POST /calculate`)

Sections in the request body:

- `A` general (`horizon`)
- `B` CapEx investments
- `C1` direct costs (per product, per unit)
- `C2` indirect costs (with periodicity)
- `D`  admin costs (with periodicity)
- `E`  products/services (price, quantity, growth, seasonality)
- `F`  personnel (salary, count, growth, type, monthsPerYear)
- `G`  financing (`creditRate`)

Rules when editing:

- Any change to request/response shape must be applied in **both**
  `server.js` and `public/script.js` in the same edit.
- Monetary results are returned in **thousands of roubles** (divided by
  1000). Never mix units silently.
- Dates come as Russian strings like `"январь 2026"`. Use the existing
  `parseRussianDate` / `RUSSIAN_MONTHS` helpers — do not duplicate them.
- Insurance premiums are 30% of payroll, net profit is 80% of EBITDA,
  discount rate is 16%. If you change any of these constants, document
  the reason in `CHANGELOG.md` and reflect user-visible impact in
  `README.md`.

## Editing Workflow

For every substantive change:

1. Make the code change.
2. Append an entry to `CHANGELOG.md` under `[Unreleased] - YYYY-MM-DD`,
   using `Added` / `Changed` / `Fixed` / `Removed`. Focus on **why**,
   not the diff. Reference touched files in backticks. Never rewrite
   historical entries — only append.
3. If the change affects run command, dependencies, project structure,
   public API of `/calculate`, or user-visible features, update the
   relevant section of `README.md` (Russian). Keep README ≤ ~250 lines
   and link to `CHANGELOG.md` for history.
4. Skip steps 2–3 only for pure formatting, typo fixes, or trivial doc
   edits.

## Code Style

- JS: `camelCase` identifiers, `const`/`let` (no `var`), early returns,
  guard clauses for invalid input (mirror existing helpers like
  `parseRussianDate` returning `null`).
- Numerical edge cases: validate IRR/NPV inputs, refuse absurd results
  (existing pattern: `IRR` bounded to `[-99%, +1000%]`).
- Comments only when intent is non-obvious. No narrating comments
  (`// increment counter`).
- Keep functions in `server.js` pure where possible (input -> output);
  side effects only in the route handler.

## Frontend Conventions (`public/`)

- HTML stays semantic and Russian-labelled.
- Do not move logic from `script.js` into inline `<script>` blocks.
- Charts use Chart.js v4 already loaded; xlsx export uses xlsx-js-style.
  Reuse existing helpers instead of adding new libraries.

## Visual Feature Governance

- Every new visual feature must pass validation against
  `.cursor/context/visual-regulator.md`.
- Mandatory execution rule:
  `.cursor/rules/visual-validation.mdc` (always apply).
- For UI changes in `public/index.html`, `public/style.css`, and visual parts
  of `public/script.js`, confirm:
  - component consistency with existing button/card/input/table/modal patterns;
  - token usage through CSS variables (avoid unnecessary hardcoded colors);
  - parity in light and dark theme;
  - no layout regressions (overflow/clipping/sidebar-topbar structure);
  - visible hover/focus states and Russian user-facing copy.

## Safety

- Never commit secrets, `.env`, or contents of `zipFile.zip`.
- Do not run destructive git commands (force push, hard reset, history
  rewrite) without explicit user approval.
- Do not auto-create commits unless asked.

## Files of Interest

- `server.js` — Express app + financial model.
- `public/index.html`, `public/script.js`, `public/style.css` — UI.
- `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` — granular rules (global conventions, change
  context, README maintenance, scope discipline, lead agent,
  developer-tester agent). This `.cursorrules` is the global summary;
  `.mdc` files contain the same policy split by concern.
- `CHANGELOG.md` — persistent change context (English).
- `README.md` — current-state documentation (Russian).
